    solution: 'SELECT user_id, COUNT(*) AS order_count\nFROM orders\nGROUP BY user_id\nHAVING COUNT(*) >= 2\nORDER BY order_count DESC',
    debrief: 'Eleven users are repeat buyers. User 5 (eve) leads with 5 orders, including three consecutive days in January 2024. HAVING filters after aggregation — it is the only way to filter on aggregated values. WHERE COUNT(*) >= 2 is a syntax error because WHERE runs before GROUP BY and has no access to the aggregated count. Users 13–15 have no orders at all and correctly do not appear.\n\n**Wrong answer (runs, measures the wrong thing):** omitting HAVING COUNT(*) >= 2 → returns ALL users with any orders (12 users including one-time buyers). User 1 with 1 order appears as a "repeat buyer" — the retention team misclassifies single-purchase users, inflates the repeat-buyer metric, and under-invests in re-engagement. No error. 12 rows instead of 11. **Catch it:** result should have 11 rows. SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT user_id) FROM orders WHERE user_id IN (your result) AND user_id NOT IN (SELECT user_id FROM orders GROUP BY user_id HAVING COUNT(*) >= 2) should return 0. If it returns > 0, you\'re including one-time buyers.\n\n**Sanity check:** MIN(order_count) in your result should be 2. If it\'s 1, HAVING is missing. SELECT MIN(order_count) FROM (your query) = 2 ✅.',
