    solution: 'SELECT u.user_id, u.email, SUM(a.balance) AS total_balance\nFROM accounts a\nJOIN users u ON a.user_id = u.user_id\nWHERE a.status = \'active\'\nGROUP BY u.user_id, u.email\nORDER BY total_balance DESC',
    debrief: 'Carol (user 3) leads with $33,750 — $8,750 in checking and $25,000 in an investment account. Alice (user 1) has $17,420.50 split across two accounts. Users 4 (frozen account) and 9 (closed account) return no rows because their accounts fail the WHERE status = \'active\' filter — they are correctly absent, not an oversight. SUM differs from COUNT here: COUNT(*) would give the number of accounts per user, SUM(balance) gives the total value. Both are GROUP BY + aggregate, but SUM aggregates a numeric measure, not row counts. The WHERE before GROUP BY pre-filters the rows; you could also use HAVING SUM(a.balance) > 0 after grouping, but pre-filtering is more efficient.\n\n**Wrong answer (runs, measures the wrong thing):** omitting WHERE a.status = \'active\' → user 4 (dan, frozen account at $9,500) and user 9 (iris, closed account at $2,100) appear in the net-worth summary. Finance presents $9,500 as accessible wealth for a frozen account. The product shows the wrong balance to the user. **Catch it:** result should have exactly 10 rows. If you get 12, the inactive accounts are included. Cross-check: SELECT DISTINCT a.status FROM accounts a JOIN your_result r ON a.user_id = r.user_id — should return only \'active\'.\n\n**Sanity check:** SUM of all total_balance in your result = SELECT SUM(balance) FROM accounts WHERE status = \'active\'. If they don\'t match, your filter or JOIN is wrong.',
