    solution: 'SELECT a.company_name, a.employee_count, p.name AS plan_name, s.mrr\nFROM accounts a\nJOIN subscriptions s ON a.account_id = s.account_id\nJOIN plans p ON s.plan_id = p.plan_id\nWHERE a.employee_count >= 100\n  AND s.status = \'active\'\nORDER BY a.employee_count DESC',
    debrief: 'Five large accounts are on active subscriptions. Three (Echo Tech, Kilo Pharma, Acme Corp) are already on Enterprise — highest tier, no upsell headroom. Capitol Finance (200 employees, 200-seat Growth plan at $299) and Golf Logistics (120 employees, Business at $999) are the real expansion targets. Mike Manufacturing and November SaaS have 500 and 100 employees respectively but no active subscription — they churned. The WHERE clause has two conditions from two different tables: employee_count from accounts, status from subscriptions. Both must be true simultaneously. A single-table filter on accounts alone would not exclude churned accounts — the subscription status join is required.\n\n**Wrong answer (runs, measures the wrong thing):** omitting AND s.status = \'active\' → Mike Manufacturing (500 employees, churned at $999 MRR) and November SaaS (100 employees, churned) appear in the expansion list. The sales team calls churned accounts as expansion targets. Pipeline is inflated. Trust is broken. **Catch it:** result should have 5 rows. If you get 7, churned accounts are included. Run SELECT DISTINCT s.status FROM subscriptions s JOIN your_result r ON s.account_id = r.account_id — should return only \'active\'.\n\n**Sanity check:** every company in your result should map to a currently active subscription. The highest employee_count in your result should be Kilo Pharma or Echo Tech at 300. If you see 500 (Mike Manufacturing), the status filter is missing.',
