    hints: ['is_premium is 0 or 1. SUM(is_premium) gives the count of premium users. Divide by COUNT(*) for the rate.'],
    checkValues: [{ device_os: 'ios', total_users: '6', premium_users: '3' }],
    solution: 'SELECT device_os,\n  COUNT(*) AS total_users,\n  SUM(is_premium) AS premium_users,\n  ROUND(100.0 * SUM(is_premium) / COUNT(*), 1) AS premium_pct\nFROM users\nGROUP BY device_os\nORDER BY premium_pct DESC',
    debrief: 'iOS and web both convert at 50% premium; Android converts at only 20%. iOS has the highest absolute premium count (3 users) and web is close behind (2 of 4). SUM(is_premium) works because is_premium is a binary 0/1 column — summing it gives the count of 1s, which is the count of premium users. AVG(is_premium) is numerically equivalent here but hides the intent: the explicit SUM/COUNT form makes the formula readable and generalises to any threshold, not just 0/1. This is the same rate calculation pattern as session conversion rate — the SQL shape is identical, only the column and business context change.\n\n**Before writing:** confirm the denominator. "Premium rate" here = premium users / all users per platform. An alternative is premium users / users who have completed the paywall flow — a smaller, more qualified denominator that produces a higher (and more honest) rate for the platform monetisation team. Both are valid. The choice changes which platform "wins."\n\n**Wrong answer (runs, measures the wrong thing):** ROUND(SUM(is_premium) / COUNT(*), 1) — missing 100.0. Integer division: 3/6 = 0 in SQL. Every platform returns premium_pct = 0.0. The revenue team sees zero premium penetration across all platforms and escalates to leadership. **Catch it:** iOS should show 50.0. If it shows 0.0, the 100.0 multiplier is missing.\n\n**Sanity check:** manually verify iOS: SELECT SUM(is_premium), COUNT(*) FROM users WHERE device_os = \'ios\' → should return 3 premium, 6 total → 50.0%.',
