    solution: 'SELECT sub_id, account_id, plan_id, started_at, ended_at, mrr\nFROM subscriptions\nWHERE status = \'churned\'\nORDER BY ended_at',
    debrief: 'Five churned subscriptions. Account 1 churned its original Business plan ($999 MRR) but then re-subscribed at Enterprise ($2,999) — so the churn is deceptive. Account 3 downgraded rather than churned outright (still active on Growth). Accounts 13, 14, 15 are genuine churn. Always check whether a churned account has a subsequent active subscription before classifying them as lost. Two approaches to identify true churn vs. re-subscription: Approach A — LEFT JOIN: filter status = \'churned\', then LEFT JOIN back to subscriptions on account_id WHERE sub2.status = \'active\' and keep only rows where sub2.sub_id IS NULL (accounts with no active sub anywhere). Approach B — NOT EXISTS: WHERE status = \'churned\' AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM subscriptions s2 WHERE s2.account_id = s1.account_id AND s2.status = \'active\'). NOT EXISTS is the cleaner production form — it short-circuits on the first match found rather than scanning all rows.\n\n**Wrong answer (runs, measures the wrong thing):** treating all 5 rows as "lost customers" for a win-back campaign. Account 1 (Acme Corp) is in this result but is already on your highest-tier active plan at $2,999 MRR. A win-back email goes to your best current customer. Credibility lost. **Catch it:** before any analysis on churned subscriptions, run SELECT s_churned.account_id, s_active.sub_id FROM your_result LEFT JOIN subscriptions s_active ON s_active.account_id = your_result.account_id AND s_active.status = \'active\'. Any row with a non-NULL s_active.sub_id is a re-subscriber, not a lost customer.\n\n**Sanity check:** your 5 rows + SELECT COUNT(*) FROM subscriptions WHERE status = \'active\' + any paused = SELECT COUNT(*) FROM subscriptions. Verify the total adds up to the full subscription table.',
