    datamartId: 'consumer',
    prompt: 'The growth team tracks time-to-activation — how many days elapsed between a user joining and making their first content interaction. Return user_id, username, joined_at, first_interaction_at, and days_to_first_engagement (as an integer) for all users who have made at least one interaction. Order by days_to_first_engagement ascending to surface the fastest-activating users first.',
    expectedColumns: ['user_id', 'username', 'joined_at', 'first_interaction_at', 'days_to_first_engagement'],
    expectedRowCount: 13,
    hints: ['Start with a CTE that finds each user\'s MIN(occurred_at) from the interactions table. Then JOIN that to users and use JULIANDAY(first_interaction_at) - JULIANDAY(joined_at) for the day difference. CAST to INTEGER to avoid decimals.'],
    checkValues: [{ user_id: '13', username: 'mike', days_to_first_engagement: '114' }],
    solution: 'WITH first_interaction AS (\n  SELECT user_id, MIN(occurred_at) AS first_interaction_at\n  FROM interactions\n  GROUP BY user_id\n)\nSELECT u.user_id, u.username, u.joined_at, fi.first_interaction_at,\n  CAST(JULIANDAY(fi.first_interaction_at) - JULIANDAY(u.joined_at) AS INTEGER) AS days_to_first_engagement\nFROM users u\nJOIN first_interaction fi ON u.user_id = fi.user_id\nORDER BY days_to_first_engagement ASC',
    debrief: 'Mike (user 13, joined 2023-08-01) activated fastest at 114 days — his only interaction was a view on content 4 on 2023-11-23. Most users joined months before interactions started in November 2023. The CTE separates the aggregation (MIN occurred_at per user) from the final calculation — without it you cannot reference first_interaction_at in JULIANDAY without nesting. JULIANDAY() returns a floating-point Julian Day number; subtracting two values gives the difference in days. CAST(...AS INTEGER) truncates the decimal since all dates here are day-level. The 13-row result excludes users 14 and 15 who have no interactions — a LEFT JOIN would include them with NULL days_to_first_engagement, which you want if the question is "what fraction of users have activated at all." The weak answer subtracts date strings directly — SQLite does not support date subtraction natively; JULIANDAY is the correct idiom.',
    sqliteNote: 'Uses JULIANDAY() for date arithmetic — SQLite-specific. PostgreSQL equivalent: DATE_PART(\'day\', first_interaction_at::date - joined_at::date).',
