    solution: 'WITH monthly AS (\n  SELECT strftime(\'%Y-%m\', created_at) AS order_month,\n    COUNT(*) AS order_count\n  FROM orders\n  GROUP BY order_month\n)\nSELECT order_month, order_count,\n  LAG(order_count) OVER (ORDER BY order_month) AS prev_month_count,\n  order_count - LAG(order_count) OVER (ORDER BY order_month) AS mom_change\nFROM monthly\nORDER BY order_month',
    debrief: 'January 2024 is the strongest month at 4 orders — up 2 from December 2023\'s 2 orders — driven by user 5\'s 3-day consecutive order streak. The first month in the dataset (2023-02) returns NULL for prev_month_count and mom_change because LAG has no prior row to reference. The CTE is essential: without materializing the monthly aggregation, you cannot reference order_count inside LAG in the same SELECT — you would have to repeat the COUNT(*) expression twice. Some databases allow referencing a column alias inside a window function call, but SQLite requires the full expression in both LAG calls. The weak answer runs two separate GROUP BY queries and manually subtracts in application code — correct but does not generalize to rolling windows or N-month lookback patterns.',
    sqliteNote: 'Uses strftime() for month extraction and LAG() window function — both supported in SQLite 3.25+.',
