    debrief: 'All 15 users appear — users 14 and 15 land in quartile 1 with zero interactions, flagging them as dormant and highest priority for re-engagement. NTILE(4) distributes rows as evenly as possible: with 15 rows, the distribution is 4,4,4,3 (the smaller group is last). This means users with the same interaction count can land in different quartiles — user 4 (2 interactions) is in Q1, while user 12 (also 2 interactions) is in Q3 based purely on row position. This mechanical splitting is NTILE\'s trade-off: it guarantees equal group sizes, not equal-value boundaries. If equal-value boundaries matter, use CASE WHEN thresholds instead. The LEFT JOIN is essential — INNER JOIN would drop users 14 and 15 entirely, silently omitting your most at-risk segment. The CTE materializes the per-user count so NTILE can reference interaction_count without recomputing it.',
