    roles: ['PA', 'DA', 'PM'],
    priority: 1,
    estimatedMin: 12,
    datamartId: 'consumer',
    prompt: 'The creator partnerships team benchmarks each creator\'s average interactions per piece of content against the platform average. Return creator_id, piece_count, avg_per_piece (rounded to 2 decimal places), and platform_avg (the overall average across all creators, rounded to 2 decimal places). Order by avg_per_piece descending.',
    expectedColumns: ['creator_id', 'piece_count', 'avg_per_piece', 'platform_avg'],
    expectedRowCount: 6,
    hints: ['Build a CTE that LEFT JOINs content to interactions and computes avg interactions per piece per creator. Then use AVG(avg_per_piece) OVER () — a window with no PARTITION BY — to compute the platform average alongside each creator row.'],
    checkValues: [{ creator_id: '1', piece_count: '2', avg_per_piece: '6.0', platform_avg: '3.42' }],
    solution: 'WITH creator_stats AS (\n  SELECT c.creator_id,\n    COUNT(DISTINCT c.content_id) AS piece_count,\n    CAST(COUNT(i.interaction_id) AS REAL) / COUNT(DISTINCT c.content_id) AS avg_per_piece\n  FROM content c\n  LEFT JOIN interactions i ON c.content_id = i.content_id\n  GROUP BY c.creator_id\n)\nSELECT creator_id, piece_count,\n  ROUND(avg_per_piece, 2) AS avg_per_piece,\n  ROUND(AVG(avg_per_piece) OVER (), 2) AS platform_avg\nFROM creator_stats\nORDER BY avg_per_piece DESC',
    debrief: 'Creator 1 (2 pieces, avg 6.0) outperforms the platform average of 3.42 by 75%. Creators 8 and 10 (avg 1.0 each) are significantly below — both have single pieces with low engagement. The key technique: AVG(avg_per_piece) OVER () with no PARTITION BY creates a global window that spans all rows, so every row in the result carries the same platform average. This eliminates a second query or subquery just to get the denominator. CAST(COUNT(interaction_id) AS REAL) prevents integer division — in SQLite, COUNT/COUNT without casting returns 0 for any ratio less than 1. The LEFT JOIN ensures creators with zero interactions appear (none here, but important for production). Weak answer: run a separate SELECT AVG(...) and hard-code or reference the scalar — does not generalize and breaks when data changes.',
    sqliteNote: null,
