    debrief: 'Dr. Smith (provider 1) has the lowest completion rate at 60% — 4 of 10 appointments were no-shows. The JOIN to providers is required to retrieve name and specialty; appointments only has provider_id. GROUP BY must include all non-aggregated columns (provider_id, name, specialty) — omitting name or specialty would fail in strict SQL dialects. SUM(CASE WHEN no_show = 0 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) counts completed appointments; SUM(no_show) counts no-shows — these should add to COUNT(*). The 100.0 multiplier prevents integer division: without it, any completion_rate below 1.0 would return 0. SUM(no_show) works because no_show is a binary integer column (0 or 1). A weak answer uses two separate subqueries — one for total, one for completed — joined to providers. That requires 3-table composition and is harder to audit. One GROUP BY pass is cleaner.',
